No, this will be true only in the limit of phi going to infinity, where both random variable will be Poisson distributed and therefore their sum will also be Poisson.

See for instance when phi=0.1

set.seed(12)
phi = 0.1
mu1 = 1
mu2 = 10
first_ = rnbinom(1e6, phi, mu=mu1) + rnbinom(1e6, phi, mu=mu2)
second_ = rnbinom(1e6, 2*phi, mu=mu1+mu2)

mean(first_)
mean(second_)
var(first_)
var(second_)
mean(first_**3)
mean(second_**3)
quantile(first_)
quantile(second_)

> mean(first_)

[1] 11.03025
> mean(second_)
[1] 10.98656
> var(first_)
[1] 1027.162
> var(second_)
[1] 613.6651
> mean(first_**3)
[1] 240507.6
> mean(second_**3)
[1] 89080.94

> quantile(first_)
  0%  25%  50%  75% 100%

   0    0    1    6  902

> quantile(second_)
  0%  25%  50%  75% 100%

   0    0    1   10  653

Now look how similar they are when phi=100

set.seed(12)
phi = 100
mu1 = 1
mu2 = 10
first_ = rnbinom(1e6, phi, mu=mu1) + rnbinom(1e6, phi, mu=mu2)
second_ = rnbinom(1e6, 2*phi, mu=mu1+mu2)

mean(first_)
mean(second_)
var(first_)
var(second_)
mean(first_**3)
mean(second_**3)
quantile(first_)
quantile(second_)

> mean(first_)

[1] 10.99724
> mean(second_)
[1] 10.99785
> var(first_)
[1] 12.02925
> var(second_)
[1] 11.62556
> mean(first_**3)
[1] 1741.239
> mean(second_**3)
[1] 1726.74

> quantile(first_)
  0%  25%  50%  75% 100%

   0    9   11   13   32

> quantile(second_)
  0%  25%  50%  75% 100%

   0    9   11   13   33

