>
>> On Apr 7, 2017, at 5:55 AM, Ole Petter Hansen <olepetter...@outlook.com> wrote:
>>
>> Hello everyone!
>>
>> I have the following model:
>>
>> data{
>> int N;
>> int M;
>> int K;
>> matrix[N,M] z_measurements  ;
>> matrix[N,K] X   ;
>> vector[N] Y;
>> }
>>
>> parameters{
>> vector[N] z;
>> vector[K] beta;
>> real alpha;
>> real<lower=0> sigma_y;
>> real<lower=0> sigma_z;
>> }
>>
>>
>> model{
>> for (i in 1:N)
>> z_measurements[i,] ~ normal(z[i],sigma_z);
>>
>> Y ~normal(X*beta+alpha*z,sigma_y);
>> }
>>
>> The point is that I identify some individual parameters (z) through one module, and then use these parameters in another module. The object of interest is primarily the beta-vector and the alpha-parameter. In reality my model is more complex, where the individual measurements come from ordinal models.
>>
>> So my problem is the following: it takes a long time to estimate this model (several days),

How many iterations is that and what's the size of the data?

Is there a reason you don't want to put priors on any of the
parameters?  Are any of the scales bumping up against zero or
running off to infinity or are any of the other parameters
running away?

>> and I want to run sensitivity analyses by e.g. changing the variables that enter the X-matrix. However, the z-parameters shouldn't change when I make changes to X.

I'm not sure why you'd think this.  z and X are both parts
of the formula for the mean.

>> So - have someone tried a two-step version of such a model? I.e. where we use the posterior distributions of z, and take this as data for the second step where I identify beta and alpha?

There's not a good way to do this in general.  In conjugate
models it can work.

- Bob

