Message-ID: <0rZKv0QieR4.Gpj2jo64AQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <0rZKv0QieR4.ck2QkVi3AQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <0rZKv0QieR4.-CNvhvOzAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <0rZKv0QieR4.ck2QkVi3AQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/0rZKv0QieR4/m/Gpj2jo64AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

inv_logit itself isn't very stable, as it's just this:

  inv_logit(x) = 1 / (1 + exp(-x))

No way to stop that exp from overflowing or rounding under
to zero.  Around x = -700 and we round to zero, and we should
round to 1 even sooner on the postive x side, probably around 25 or
30 or so.

What we may need in the RNG is something that tests for whether
the argument is 0 or 1 and just returns a string of those results.

- bob

