I have data comprised of 1,000 time observations across ~150 locations, and I'd like to estimate intercepts for each location. The data appear bi-modal and log-normal:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-3EzGDNm194g/WO6RKrGh_BI/AAAAAAAAAQo/OHntmMTVqy8vLmDnrAw_bCNvJKKTJDAfgCLcB/s1600/Distribution.png>

I believe a finite mixture model would be appropriate in this case. I followed along with Jim Savage's blog post here <http://modernstatisticalworkflow.blogspot.com/2016/10/finite-mixture-models-in-stan.html>, but I'm not sure how to go from the mixture parameter estimation to the HLM intercepts. My raw data is attached, and the Stan mixture code I've been using is below. Any guidance would be appreciated.

// saved as finite_mixture_linear_regression.stan
data {
  int N;
  vector[N] y;
  int n_groups;
}
parameters {
  ordered[n_groups] mu;
  vector<lower = 0>[n_groups] sigma;
  simplex[n_groups] Theta;
}
model {
  vector[n_groups] contributions;
  // priors
  mu ~ normal(0, 10);
  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 3);
  Theta ~ dirichlet(rep_vector(2.0, n_groups));

  // likelihood
  for(i in 1:N) {
    for(k in 1:n_groups) {
      contributions[k] = log(Theta[k]) + normal_lpdf(y[i] | mu[k], sigma[k]);
    }
    target += log_sum_exp(contributions);
  }
}

