>> matrix M,
>> int[,,] ht_select, int[,,,] ht_gmap, int[,] Ht,
>> matrix Z, int[] Z_type, vector Zdvec, int[,] Zdmat_segments)
>>
>> There are a whole string of user-defined functions sitting behind this, the details of which I assume aren't especially relevant, but you can roughly think of each element of R as a kernel regression prediction of M|Z, based on a somewhat awkward subset of the other observations, and where the lambda parameters control the bandwidths of a K_lambda-dimensional gaussian kernel. The underlying code involves a lot more looping than I'd ideally like, but more efficient versions using matrix operations are unfortunately too memory intensive.
>>
>> The returned matrix R is NT x K_rho (with NT  ~50-100k and K_rho typically only 1 or 2), lambda is a parameter vector (with typical length ~5-10), and all the other arguments are either data, or transformed data (mainly arrays of indices designed to deal with the aforementioned awkward subsets).
>>
>> If we just focus on this function, then (in addition to the R matrix), I assume I will also need to be able to return d R[i,j] / d lambda[k] for all i,j,k (~500k-2m terms), which should be feasible. Is that right?
>>
>> Glenn.
>>
>> On 17 April 2017 at 17:52, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
>> Hi Glenn,
>>
>> If you know how to compute the function value and the gradients with respect to each argument of the function that could possibly be a Stan parameter (or a function of one), then you can write a function in C++ that will add a single expression to the expression graph. We can walk you through that if you can describe the function better. At least what arguments it has and which can be Stan parameters. Before digging too deep, we should make sure you can compute enough gradients that are required.
>>
>> Regarding memory usage, my guess is that it's actually not the autodiff that's blowing out memory. I think there's a way to steam the output to disk without storing in memory in RStan, but if there isn't, try running it in CmdStan just to see how much memory it actually takes. If you haven't vectorized the program, it might also help to do that.
>>
>>
>> Daniel
>>
>>
