> However, the way I ended defining the double specialisation was somewhat unsatisfactory: I just copied wholesale the var specialisation and then manually removed all the pieces that weren't related to the gradient calculations. This isn't great from a maintainability perspective, as the code is 80% identical, with only 4 short code chunks that aren't needed in the double version (a set of declarations; extraction of the var elements into a double for further calculations; calculation of the gradient matrix; and population and return of the stored_gradient_vari).

We usually have the autodiff version call the double version when we do that.
Or you can break out functions.

>
> I'd ideally like (and thought I should be able) to have a single templated version of the code, just with conditional execution of the gradient/var-related bits.

That's challenging.  It's what we do with our pdf and pmf implementations, so have a look at the normal_lpdf implementation.  But it involves a lot of template metaprogramming to not run afoul of the kinds of problems you run into (that everything needs to be instantiatable).

Sean's busy cleaning up the infrastructure in operators_and_partials so
that'll be a cleaner piece of the puzzle in 2.16.

> However, my naive attempt to do this using type traits didn't work. In particular, e.g.:
