> After perusing the mailing list yesterday, I think I've come to realize that we're dealing with the convolution of multiple random variables. It sounds like we might have to wait for the fast Fourier transforms to be implemented to try to do the numerical integration.

Hopefully we'll get that out soon.

> For now, I think my two choices are either to use the Normal distribution which would allow us to account for correlation but not right-skew or strict non-negativity or to use the Gamma distribution with a common rate parameter and assume independence of travel times. We're going to move forward with the Gamma distribution because it's better than what we've got in JAGS.
>
> Just to clarify using your notation below: sometimes we observe only Z, but for some individuals we observe both X and Y. To speak to your suggestions, I don't think the latent variable approach will work within a general multi-state framework. There are two related reasons for this. First, we have to keep track of the number of unobserved travel times because I've discovered Stan does not like imputing a variable that will not be used later.

The real issue is that Stan requires a proper posterior.  So if
that imputed variable introduces impropriety, you're hosed.  It's
still a problem with JAGS, but it's slower to mix.  If you put a prior
on that imputed variable, it should be OK.  But then there's no reason
to impute a variable that isn't used later (at least for some kind of
posterior predictive inference).

> Second, I don't see a way to work with multiple latent variables within a multistate model.

It depends on how they're structured.  If they're continuous,
you just need to build up the combined structure of latent and observed
variables as outlined in the missing data chapter of the manual.
If they're discrete, they need to be marginalized out.  We have examples
for the CJS model, general HMMs, both of which marginalize out multiple
discrete parameters.

> I got as far as figuring out how to do the latent variable approach within a simple three-occasion, three state model (based off the BPA example code on the Stan github page).

Hiroki Ito wrote those and I'm not sure he monitors our users list.
You might want to drop him a note---he may have already thought about
some of this.

> That code is attached. In the model, I assume that travel time from occasion to occasion is simply based on what the state the individual was in during the last occasion.
>
> The key addition to the basic multistate model is:
>
>   miss_count = 1;
>
>   for (i in 1:N) {
>     if (y[i,2] != 3){
>       obs_cuml_trav_time[i,2] ~ lognormal(mu[y[i,1]], sigma[y[i,1]]);
>       if (y[i,3] !=3){
>         real diff;
>         diff = obs_cuml_trav_time[i,3] - obs_cuml_trav_time[i,2];
>         diff ~ lognormal(mu[y[i,2]], sigma[y[i,2]]);
>       }
>     } else { //y[i,2] == 3
>       if (y[i,3] !=3){
>         real diff;
>         tt_1_missing[miss_count] ~ lognormal(mu[y[i,1]], sigma[y[i,1]]);
>         diff = obs_cuml_trav_time[i,3] - tt_1_missing[miss_count];
>         miss_count = miss_count + 1;
>         if (y[i,1] == 1)
>           target += log_sum_exp(log(1 - psiAB[1]) + lognormal_lpdf(diff | mu[1], sigma[1]), //probability fish was in state 1 during time 2
>                                 log(psiAB[1]) + lognormal_lpdf(diff | mu[2], sigma[2]));//probability fish was in state 1 during time 2
>         if (y[i,1] ==2)
>           target += log_sum_exp(log(psiBA[1]) + lognormal_lpdf(diff | mu[1], sigma[1]), //probability fish was in state 1 during time 2
>                                 log(1 - psiBA[1]) + lognormal_lpdf(diff | mu[2], sigma[2]));//probability fish was in state 1 during time 2
>       }
>     }
>   }
>
> But this becomes intractable when you get beyond three occasions because we'd need a discrete latent variable to keep track of which state the individual was actually in to avoid impossible sums.

Intratable sums?  How many values can these variables take on?    If you have a vector
of latent variables x in p(x, y), then you can always do p(y) = SUM_x p(x, y), or
in Stan, log p(y) = log_sum_exp_{x} log p(x, y), but it gets intractable if there are
too many instantiations of x in the summation.

- Bob

