Hey Bob,

Thanks again for your reply. But I think there is a statistical issue here that goes beyond the coding issue. I may be wrong, and if one of the more heady stats folks see this, hopefully they can correct me but I think what I want in this circumstance is the conditional model not the marginal model for Z[4]. That is, if I code this up as you are suggesting by imputing X[2] and X[3] as marginal then Z[4] = X[1] + X[2] + X[3] will not give the same probability model as imputing them conditionally. I'm pretty sure, we would need to keep track of the discrete latent state which Stan cannot do (currently). Let me outline my intuition.

The mixture of uniforms I was using in my earlier reply was simply a crude arbitrary example to illustrate my intuition. Instead lets consider the same example but use a mixture of Normal distributions because Normal is easier to work with analytically.

Just a reminder here is the scenario:
We are considering the capture history AXXB (so middle two states are unobserved). We know that individuals can only transition states between occasion 1 and 2 and between occasion 3 and 4 (e.g. psiAB[2] = psiBA[2] = 0). So our unobserved capture history can only be AAAB or ABBB. We keep our assumption that travel time between occasion t and occasion t+1 is dependent only on the state the individual was in during occasion t. The capture history AAAB would have interoccasion travel times of X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ foo(alpha), X[3] ~ foo(alpha) and ABBB would have travel times of X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ bar(beta), X[3] ~ bar(beta). AAAB occurs with probability (1 - psiAB) and ABBB with probability psiAB. We are interested in the distribution of Z, where Z = X[1] + X[2] + X[3].

Instead of foo(alpha) and bar(beta), lets have two Normal distributions. N(mu_A, sig_A^2) and N(mu_B, sig_B^2). Using the very convenient fact that the sum of independent Normal distributions is Normally distributed we have that Z ~ (1 - psiAB)* N(mu_A +  mu_A + muA, sig_A ^2+  sig_A^2 +  sig_A^2) + (psiAB) * N(mu_A +  mu_B + muB, sig_A^2 +  sig_B^2 +  sig_B^2). This is nice simple mixture distribution of two Normals. Assuming arbitrary values for the mixture proportion and the means and standard deviations we can simulate this process in R. Here I simulate the sum of three mixed normal where the state only changes between the first and second occasion. That is, for each simulated X[2] and X[3] are both drawn from the same mixture component based on the latent state. In other words X[3] is conditional on X[2] or rather both are condition on the underlying state during occasion 2. I believe this to be an adequate model for the biological process we want to describe and so following the practice of modelling as you would simulate here is how I would simulate the data in R:

N = 1000
psiAB_1 = .3
mu_A = 3
sig_A = 1.25
mu_B = 7
sig_B = 0.75

X_cond = matrix(rep(NA, N*3), ncol = 3)
Z_cond = rep(NA, N)

for (i in 1:N){

  X_cond[i,1] = rnorm(1, mean = mu_A, sd = sig_A)
  state = rbinom(1, size = 1, psiAB_1)
  X_cond[i,2] = (state == 0) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_A, sd = sig_A) +
                (state == 1) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_B, sd = sig_B)
  X_cond[i,3] = (state == 0) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_A, sd = sig_A) +
                (state == 1) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_B, sd = sig_B)
  Z_cond[i] = X_cond[i,1] + X_cond[i,2] + X_cond[i,3]
}

plot(density(Z_cond))

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-1SWMG7pvmsA/WP5-qBerYsI/AAAAAAAAKJw/I6ndPyNsOSg0sVEAVVSy6C4X5-no5dlDgCLcB/s1600/Rplot.jpeg>

We get the same distribution by working directly with the marginal distribution of sum:

Z = rep(NA, N)

for (i in 1:N){

  state = rbinom(1, size = 1, psiAB_1)
  Z[i] = (state == 0) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_A + mu_A + mu_A, sd = sqrt(sig_A^2 + sig_A^2 + sig_A^2)) +
         (state == 1) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_A + mu_B + mu_B, sd = sqrt(sig_A^2 + sig_B^2 + sig_B^2))
}
plot(density(Z))

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-dj5dT2Ja8xs/WP6DIdd05ZI/AAAAAAAAKJ8/LWYbRv1peAo9xEDBe9x6_8k5R7zkOWm_QCLcB/s1600/Rplot01.jpeg>

But if instead we simulate the process where we sum X[1:3] where X[3] is determined by it's marginal distribution (i.e. independent of X[2]) we get a different distribution. This is the simulation that matches the Stan probability we describe below where we impute the marginal distribution of each component of the sum.

X_marg = matrix(rep(NA, N*3), ncol = 3)
Z_marg = rep(NA, N)

for (i in 1:N){

  X_marg[i,1] = rnorm(1, mean = mu_A, sd = sig_A)
  state = rbinom(1, size = 1, psiAB_1)
  X_marg[i,2] = (state == 0) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_A, sd = sig_A) +
    (state == 1) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_B, sd = sig_B)
  newstate = rbinom(1, size = 1, psiAB_1) #HERE newstate ensures that X[,3] is ignorant of information in X[,2]
  X_marg[i,3] = (newstate == 0) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_A, sd = sig_A) +
    (newstate == 1) * rnorm(1, mean = mu_B, sd = sig_B)
  Z_marg[i] = X_marg[i,1] + X_marg[i,2] + X_marg[i,3]
}
plot(density(Z_marg))

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-RSfOjpnUJdY/WP6DP18V76I/AAAAAAAAKKA/XNZ_FvUEQ1os3iWUKNkg8sffXkr3o0jGQCLcB/s1600/Rplot02.jpeg>

One more plot verifies that X[3] simulated conditionally on the state at time 2 and X[3] simulated marginally have the identical distribution, but their sum leads to a different probability model if we use the conditional distribution for X[3] rather than the marginal.

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-oB6UI-hPrps/WP6FCux6rFI/AAAAAAAAKKM/L04M1ymkD7kFsmbeg2mSFgK4ut6DeyyWwCLcB/s1600/Rplot03.jpeg>

I attempted to verify this in Stan (code attached). If I give Z_cond to "normal_3sum_mixture_direct.stan" I am able to recover the parameters I specified in the simulation. If I give it Z_marg I get back the wrong answer (and it takes a lot longer to converge). I wasn't able to get the sum of imputed marginals to not give me a ton of divergent transitions. I didn't want to sink a bunch of time into figuring that out because I've convinced myself that by attempting to impute the marginal distribution of each occasion specific travel time I am working with a different probability model of the sum than the one I intend.

So to get back to my original question with the post I'm pretty sure I need to find a way to work with the marginal distribution of the sum directly. If I wanted to attack it by imputing hte unobserved travel times I'd need a way to keep track of the latent state for each component of the sum. Maybe there's a way to do this with a multivariate mixture model? At this point I'm going to move forward with the Gamma distribution with a fixed rate parameter. But if anybody smarter than me (basically I think that means any or or all of you) has a better idea, I'd love to try to wrap my head around it.

On Sunday, April 23, 2017 at 3:42:38 PM UTC-7, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> If the latent states are continuous, you can use parameters
> for them and let the MCMC do the marginalization.
>
> The only thing you really have to worry about
> is your constraints:
>
> > Z[1] = 0,
> > Z[2] = X[1],
> > Z[3] = X[1] + X[2],
> > Z[4] = X[1] + X[2] + X[3]
>
>
> If Z[2] and Z[3] are not observed, but Z[4] is, then
> you know:
>
>   X[1] + X[2] + X[3] = Z[4]
>
> You can parameterize this many different ways.  One
> is by taking X[1:3] to be a 3-simplex multiplied
> by Z[4].  Or you can do the stick-breaking we do for
> a simplex directly, taking X[1] in (0, Z[4]),
> X[2] in (0, Z[4] - X[1]) and so on.  You need to do the
> transform and then probably apply a Jacobian depending
> on how you're coding up priors.
>
> More below.
>
>
> > On Apr 21, 2017, at 1:09 PM, Dalton Hance <dalton...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Hey Bob,
> >
> > Let me try to illustrate my thinking on what I mean by impossible sums appearing when imputing multiple travel times.
> >
> > Consider the following. We have a 4 occasion capture history with two states (three including death) E.g. AAAB. This capture history is associated with a vector of cumulative travel time Z, with Z[1] = 0, Z[2], = X[1], Z[3] = X[1] + X[2], Z[4] = X[1] + X[2] + X[3]. X[t] is travel time from occasion t to occasion t + 1 and is distributed conditional on the state the individual is in during time t. So under this example X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ foo(alpha), X[3] ~ foo(alpha).
> >
> > Now consider the capture history AXXB. Here the individual was no observed during the second and third occasion. So Z[2] and Z[3] = NA. All we observe is the final cumulative travel time which is ostensibly the sum of three latent occasion to occasion travel times. We can impute these as follows:
> >
> > X[1] ~ foo(alpha)
> > X[2] ~ (1 - psiAB[1])*foo(alpha) + psiAB[1]*foo(alpha).
> > X[3] ~ (1 - psiAB[1])*(1 - psiAB[2])*foo(alpha) +  (1 - psiAB[1])*(psiAB[2])*bar(beta) + psiAB[1]*psiBA[2]*foo(alpha) + psiAB[1]*(1-psiBA[2])*bar(beta)
> >
>
> The fact that these are mixtures shouldn't be a problem, but the
> code will get more complex.
>
> > But imagine that movement between AB is not possible between occasion 2 and 3. So psiAB[2] = psiBA[2] = 0.
>
> This is like other state-space impossible transitions.
> Don't think it shoul be a problem.
>
> >
> > This leaves us with:
> >
> >  X[3] ~ (1 - psiAB[1])*foo(alpha) +  psiAB[1]*bar(beta)
> >
> > So let's get arbitrary for illustration purposes and say that foo(alpha) = Uniform(2, 3) and bar(beta) = Uniform(4, 5) (i.e. mutually exclusive).
>
> Do you really need those uniform(2, 3) and uniform(4, 5)?  Those
> seem like a recipe for violated constraints no matter what you're
> doing.
>
> > So under this formulation the sum 2.5 + 2.5 + 4.5 would be impossible because that means the individual was in state A during time t = 2, but in state B during time t = 3. But because we have to marginalize out the discrete latent variable (i.e. the unoberved state), nothing prevents Stan from considering this impossible sum, unless we also impute a state variable that effectively forces X[3] ~foo(alpha) if X[2] ~ foo(alpha). I'm under the impression that this is gradient breaking, but maybe I'm wrong?
>
> You just need to enforce the constraints, which are usually smooth.
>
> - Bob
>
>

