That's my issue. I'm trying to figure out how to define to the overall density. I was trying to point out that the simplex/imputation approach you initially suggested (or at least my understanding of what you suggested) does not give the density I want.

>
> > hopefully they can correct me but I think what I want in this circumstance is the conditional model not the marginal model for Z[4]. That is, if I code this up as you are suggesting by imputing X[2] and X[3] as marginal then Z[4] = X[1] + X[2] + X[3] will not give the same probability model as imputing them conditionally. I'm pretty sure, we would need to keep track of the discrete latent state which Stan cannot do (currently). Let me outline my intuition.
> >
> > The mixture of uniforms I was using in my earlier reply simply a crude arbitrary example to illustrate my intuition. Instead lets consider the same example but use a mixture of Normal distributions because Normal is easier to work with analytically.
> >
> > Just a reminder here is the scenario:
> > We are considering the capture history AXXB (so middle two states are unobserved). We know that individuals can only transition states between occasion 1 and 2 and between occasion 3 and 4 (e.g. psiAB[2] = psiBA[2] = 0). So our unobserved capture history can only be AAAB or ABBB. We keep our assumption that travel time between occasion t and occasion t+1 is dependent only on the state the individual was in during occasion t. The capture history AAAB would have interoccasion travel times of X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ foo(alpha), X[3] ~ foo(alpha) and ABBB would have travel times of X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ bar(beta), X[3] ~ bar(beta). AAAB occurs with probability (1 - psiAB) and ABBB with probability psiAB. We are interested in the distribution of Z, where Z = X[1] + X[2] + X[3].
>
> I'm having a hard time following this reasoning.  Can you just write down the
> joint density for the problem rather than talking about this kind of process?
> Or write the process out in full assuming everything is observed, then tell me
> what is and isn't observed?

My problem is that I can't write down the joint density for travel times outside of the special case of independent Normal or independent Gamma with a fixed rate parameter because sometimes I observe individual station-to-station travel times, and sometimes all I observe is the sum of 2 or more travel times. I would love to use the sum of independent lognormals (or the sum of correlated Gammas, the sum of correlated lognormals, etc.) but none of these have closed form densities.

For my very much simplified example, the basic process is that fish transition among states between discrete occasions and that each occasion is associated with continuous travel time whose distribution is conditional on the state it was in during the previous occasion.

Data
A capture history.  We have four occasions and two states. We condition on all fish being released in state A. Fish can transition between states A and B between occasions 1 and 2, and 3 and 4, but not between 2 and 3. They can die (state U) in between any occasion. Thus, under perfect detection, there are 9 possible capture histories: H  in (AAAA, AAAB, AAAU, ABBA, ABBB, ABBU, AAUU, ABUU, AUUU).

A cumulative travel time vector. Cumulative travel time since release is recorded at each occasion. Z = (0, Z[2], Z[3], Z[4]). If the fish is dead (or unobserved when we introduce that), an NA is recorded. Station-to-station travel times are derived as the difference between subsequent entries of the travel time vector such that X[1] = Z[2] - 0, X[2] = Z[3] - Z[2], X[3] = Z[4] - Z[3]. It should be obvious, but we can rewrite the cumulative travel time vector as (0, X[1], X[1] + X[2], X[1] + X[2] + X[3]).

PARAMETERS
For fully observed data, we have the following parameters.
S_A  = Pr(H(t+1) = U| H(t) = A)
S_B  = Pr(H(t+1) = U| H(t) = B)
psiAB[1] = Pr(H(2) = B |  H(1) = A)
psiAB[3] = Pr(H(4) = B |  H(3) = A)
psiBA[3] = Pr(H(4) = A |  H(3) = B)

Where, S_A = survival from occasion t to t +1 conditional on state being in state A at time t.  Similarly, for S_B.
and psiAB[1] probability of transitioning to state B from state A between occasion 2 and occasion 1. Similarly, for psiAB[3], psiBA[3].

Assuming station-to-station travel times are independently distributed Normal random variables (I DO NOT WANT to assume this for our actual applied example) we also have,
mu_A     = mean travel time for fish in state A during time t.
mu_B     = mean travel time for fish in state B during time t.
sigma_A = standard deviation of travel time for fish in state A during time t
sigma_B = standard deviation of travel time for fish in state B during time t

This is a trivial model with out worrying about unobserved data. We can use the forward algorithm to get the distribution for our capture histories, but some arbitrary examples are:
Pr(AAAB) = S_A*(1-psiAB[1])*S_A*S_A*psiAB[3]
Pr(ABBX) = S_A*psiAB[1]*S_B*(1-S_B)
Pr(AAXX) = S_A*(1-psiAB[1]*(1-S_A)

The associated travel time vectors are also trivial:
For AAAB we have Z[2] = X[1] ~ N(mu_A, sigma_A^2), Z[3] - Z[2] = X[2] ~ N(mu_A, sigma_A^2), Z[4] - Z[3] = X[3] ~ N(mu_A, sigma_A^2).
For ABBU we have Z[2] = X[1] ~ N(mu_A, sigma_A^2), Z[3] - Z[2] = X[2] ~ N(mu_B, sigma_B^2)
For AAUU we have Z[2] = X[1] ~ N(mu_A, sigma_A^2)

Adding Non-detection
If we add in non-detection during occasion 2, 3, and 4 such that fish that are unobserved can be either dead or just not detected we get an additional 12 possible capture histories. (AUAA, AUAB, AUAU, AUBA. AUBB, AUBU, AAUA, AAUB, AAUB, ABUA, ABUB, AUUA, AUUB).

We need to add in detection parameters now - p[2], p[3], p[4] - describing the probability of detection during each occasion. Determining the probability for each capture history becomes more complicated, but we can easily use the forward algorithm. I know how to do this. The issue now is determining the density for observed cumulative travel times for capture history which mean we only observe some elements of Z.

For example, for capture history AUAU we only observe Z[3]. We know it is the sum of the unobserved X[1] and X[2]. Because we're working with the Normal distribution, we can easily use Z[3] ~ N(mu_A + mu_A, sigma_A^2 + sigma_A^2). This is because we know the fish must have been in state A during occasion 2 given that we know it was in state A during occasion 3.

The most complicated cases for travel time in this toy example are AUUB and AUUA. In both cases we only observe Z[4] which we know to be the sum of unobserved X[1], X[2], X[3]. But we can still take advantage of the sum of Normals and fit this as a mixture distribution. For both AUUA and AUUA, we have Z[4] ~ (1 - psiAB[1])* N(mu_A +  mu_A + muA, sig_A ^2+  sig_A^2 +  sig_A^2) + (psiAB[1]) * N(mu_A +  mu_B + muB, sig_A^2 +  sig_B^2 +  sig_B^2).

Beyond Normality

The problem that I have is that in my applied example the assumption that each station-to-station travel time are independent Normal random variables is untenable. We have non-negative right skewed travel times. They're probably correlated (fast fish stay fast). If we had perfect detection we could simply model X[1], X[2] and X[3], but sometimes we do not observe them. For example if we wanted to model X[1] ~ Lognormal(mu_A, sigma_A^2|H(1) = A), X[2] ~ Lognormal(mu_B, sigma_B^2|H(2) = B), X[3] ~ Lognormal(mu_B, sigma_B^2|H(3) = B)  there is no closed form solution for X[1] + X[2] + X[3] for cases where all we observe is Z[4]. So I can't write that density, let alone figure out what the mixture distribution of it is.

>
> > N = 1000
> > psiAB_1 = .3
> > mu_A = 3
> > sig_A = 1.25
> > mu_B = 7
> > sig_B = 0.75
