Here X_cond and Z_cond are data, not parameters. The parameters are mu's and sigma's. I was just trying to illustrate the difference in density between summing the marginal distribution of the X's and the conditional distribution of the X's. In your original reply you seemed to imply it would suffice to impute unobserved travel times according to the marginal distributions (integrating over the unobserved state vector) and then summing them. I'm trying to illustrate that this leads to a different density than if you keep track of the state vector and impute the X's conditional. That if X[2] is drawn from the A side of the mixture distribution, we have to ensure that X[3] is also drawn from that side. I'm probably being very loose with my language though and for that I apologize.

