Can you write down the density you want as a formula?  I obviously
don't understand your other notation if that's not what you want.

More below.  Not in red.

> > hopefully they can correct me but I think what I want in this circumstance is the conditional model not the marginal model for Z[4]. That is, if I code this up as you are suggesting by imputing X[2] and X[3] as marginal then Z[4] = X[1] + X[2] + X[3] will not give the same probability model as imputing them conditionally. I'm pretty sure, we would need to keep track of the discrete latent state which Stan cannot do (currently). Let me outline my intuition.
> >
> > The mixture of uniforms I was using in my earlier reply simply a crude arbitrary example to illustrate my intuition. Instead lets consider the same example but use a mixture of Normal distributions because Normal is easier to work with analytically.
> >
> > Just a reminder here is the scenario:
> > We are considering the capture history AXXB (so middle two states are unobserved). We know that individuals can only transition states between occasion 1 and 2 and between occasion 3 and 4 (e.g. psiAB[2] = psiBA[2] = 0). So our unobserved capture history can only be AAAB or ABBB. We keep our assumption that travel time between occasion t and occasion t+1 is dependent only on the state the individual was in during occasion t. The capture history AAAB would have interoccasion travel times of X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ foo(alpha), X[3] ~ foo(alpha) and ABBB would have travel times of X[1] ~ foo(alpha), X[2] ~ bar(beta), X[3] ~ bar(beta). AAAB occurs with probability (1 - psiAB) and ABBB with probability psiAB. We are interested in the distribution of Z, where Z = X[1] + X[2] + X[3].
>
> I'm having a hard time following this reasoning.  Can you just write down the
> joint density for the problem rather than talking about this kind of process?
> Or write the process out in full assuming everything is observed, then tell me
> what is and isn't observed?
>
> My problem is that I can't write down the joint density for travel times outside of the special case of independent Normal or independent Gamma with a fixed rate parameter because sometimes I observe individual station-to-station travel times, and sometimes all I observe is the sum of 2 or more travel times. I would love to use the sum of independent lognormals (or the sum of correlated Gammas, the sum of correlated lognormals, etc.) but none of these have closed form densities.

Yes, you can write down the joint density for this.  See our
discussion of movement HMMs (or the vast literature on calculating
densities in state-space models).  The fact that some of these
outcomes are not observed is not an issue. The summing out works
exactly the same way.

>
> For my very much simplified example, the basic process is that fish transition among states between discrete occasions and that each occasion is associated with continuous travel time whose distribution is conditional on the state it was in during the previous occasion.

That sounds like a movement HMM!

>
> Data
> A capture history.  We have four occasions and two states. We condition on all fish being released in state A. Fish can transition between states A and B between occasions 1 and 2, and 3 and 4, but not between 2 and 3. They can die (state U) in between any occasion. Thus, under perfect detection, there are 9 possible capture histories: H  in (AAAA, AAAB, AAAU, ABBA, ABBB, ABBU, AAUU, ABUU, AUUU).
>
> A cumulative travel time vector. Cumulative travel time since release is recorded at each occasion. Z = (0, Z[2], Z[3], Z[4]). If the fish is dead (or unobserved when we introduce that), an NA is recorded. Station-to-station travel times are derived as the difference between subsequent entries of the travel time vector such that X[1] = Z[2] - 0, X[2] = Z[3] - Z[2], X[3] = Z[4] - Z[3]. It should be obvious, but we can rewrite the cumulative travel time vector as (0, X[1], X[1] + X[2], X[1] + X[2] + X[3]).
>
> PARAMETERS
> For fully observed data, we have the following parameters.

> S_A  = Pr(H(t+1) = X| H(t) = A)

> S_B  = Pr(H(t+1) = U| H(t) = B)
> psiAB[1] = Pr(H(2) = B |  H(1) = A)
> psiAB[3] = Pr(H(4) = B |  H(3) = A)
> psiBA[3] = Pr(H(4) = A |  H(3) = B)

Why are you setting variables equal to probabilities?  I'm having
a very hard time with the notation here.

- Bob

